#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115243 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "853a04b2-591a-51c2-b80f-73f559355d9f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Elementary 2x2 Matrix"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115244 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6c56b3e9-cf23-5e1e-8c7d-c474059c9a9d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Inverse of an Elementary 2x2 Matrix"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115245 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dd12bc7b-1b5d-5af2-bcef-c9d0fda7d8fc" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Product Involving the Inverse of an Elementary 2x2 Matrix"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116495 :tutorial/content "An **elementary matrix** is a matrix that can be obtained from the identity matrix by applying *only one* elementary row operation.\n\nFor example, starting with the $2 \\times 2$ identity matrix $I_2={\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{array} \\right]},$ we can apply a single elementary row operation to get any of the matrices below:\n\n$$\nE_1= {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{array} \\right]}, \\qquad \\quad E_2= {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{array} \\right]}, \\qquad \\quad E_3= {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & 0 \\\\ - 7 & 1\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\n- $E_1$ is obtained from $I_2$ by applying the row operation $R_1 \\leftrightarrow R_2.$\n\n- $E_2$ is obtained from $I_2$ by applying the row operation $R_1:=2R_1.$\n\n- $E_3$ is obtained from $I_2$ by applying the row operation $R_2 := R_2 + (-7)R_1.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "85735558-3e6a-5a6e-be0b-95227712e60a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3164 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116496 :tutorial/content "Each elementary matrix $E$ is invertible, and the corresponding inverse is always an elementary matrix of the same type as $E$.\nTo illustrate, let's consider the matrix \n\n$$\nE = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\nTo find the inverse of $E,$ we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1:** Write down $\\color{blue}E$ and the identity matrix $I_2$ side-by-side, as shown below:\n$$\n[{\\color{blue}{E}}\\, |\\, {\\color{red}I_2}]\t\n=\t\n\\left[{\\begin{array}{cc|cc}{\\color{blue}1} & {\\color{blue}0} & 1 & 0 \\\\ {\\color{blue}4} & {\\color{blue}1} & 0 & 1\\end{array}}\\right]\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Apply an elementary row operation that transforms the left matrix into the identity matrix. In our case, we need to apply the transformation $R_2 := R_2 + (-4)R_1.$ We apply the operation to the entire row (not only the left part):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[E\\,|\\,{I}_{2}] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} &  & {R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|cc}1 & 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & 1\\end{array}\\right] \\\\  & =[{I}_{2}\\,|\\,{E}^{-1}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $E^{-1}$ is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore,\n$$\nE^{-1} = \n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}1 & 0 \\\\ - 4 & 1\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nWe can work out the inverse of all elementary $2 \\times 2$ matrices in a similar way." :tutorial/id #uuid "24a632e9-4a94-5212-b848-8d4b4403d452" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3165 :tutorial/title "The Inverse of an Elementary Matrix"}]}
